{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And YHWH came down upon Mount Sinai</b> If you contemplate the pericah you will understand that His great Name came down upon Mount Sinai and dwelt upon it in fire, and He was speaking with Moses. And the speaking with Moses throughout the entire pericah was in the unique Name, and the ascent (above, verse 3) and the going forth (above, verse 17) were toward the place of the Glory, as I have explained (above, verse 3). And He warned <b>lest they break through to YHWH to see</b>, for even the nobles of the children of Israel did not behold Him, and all Israel heard the voice of HaShem from within the fire. And it is written (Exodus 20:1), \"And Elohim spoke all these words,\" as our Sages explained (Mekhilta d'Rabbi Yishmael), there is no Elohim but the Judge, and they said (Makkot 24a), \"From the mouth of the Almighty we heard it,\" and in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 5:19) it is written, \"These words HaShem spoke to all your assembly,\" because it specified \"from within the fire.\" And this is the meaning of \"face to face\" (Deuteronomy 5:4), and therefore it is stated, \"I am YHWH your Elohim.\" And do not be troubled by what they said to Moses, \"For who is there of all flesh, who has heard the voice of the living Elohim speaking from within the fire\" (Deuteronomy 5:21), for they did not say \"who has heard Elohim speaking from within the fire,\" but rather he said \"the voice of Elohim\" — they reported what they perceived. And therefore they said, \"You draw near and hear all that HaShem our Elohim will say\" (Deuteronomy 5:24). And likewise Moses said to them, \"Has a people ever heard the voice of Elohim speaking from within the fire?\" (Deuteronomy 4:33), for \"speaking\" is an attribute of the voice, as in the expression \"and he heard the voice speaking to him\" (Numbers 7:89).<br>From this you will understand what our Sages always said in the midreshei ha-haggadot (see Shemot Rabbah 28:6), that the Torah was given in seven voices, and these are the ones David mentioned in the psalm, \"Ascribe to YHWH, O sons of elim\" (Psalms 29:1). And this is the number alluded to in this pericah, for <b>and there were voices (קֹלֹת) and torches</b> is defective and is counted as one, and likewise <b>and all the people saw the voices (הַקּוֹלֹת)</b> — the plural waw is missing from it, and so they are six, and it has already been stated explicitly <b>and Elohim spoke</b>. And likewise in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 4:33–36) seven voices are mentioned at the giving of the Torah. And in the gemara in tractate Berakhot (6b) they said it was given in five voices — they count the hidden voices and the two that are explicit in the verse. And the intention is that to Moses our teacher it was given in seven voices, and he heard and contemplated them, but to Israel they heard it in a single voice, as it says, \"a great voice and it did not cease\" (Deuteronomy 5:19), and it is written, \"a voice of words you hear, but no form do you see, only a voice\" (Deuteronomy 4:12). And even here He hinted, <b>and all the people saw the voices (הַקּוֹלֹת)</b>, with one missing, for all the voices appeared as one. And this is what is stated (Psalms 62:12), \"One thing Elohim has spoken, two have I heard\" — by way of truth. And so the verses are explained; not a thing is exchanged for another."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "The phrase 'Mishneh Torah' here refers to the book of Deuteronomy, not Maimonides' code; Ramban uses it as the standard medieval designation for that biblical book."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:11:16.984635198Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}